  "content": {
    "id": "p12-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "Disprove each of the following existential statements:",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p12-q5-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "There exist positive integers $m$ and $n$ for which $m^2-n^2=1$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No such positive integers exist.\n\nFactoring gives $(m-n)(m+n)=1$, forcing $m-n=m+n=1$ and hence $n=0$.",
          "worked": "Suppose such positive integers exist. Since $m^2>n^2$, we have $m>n$. Thus both factors in\n$$ (m-n)(m+n)=1 $$\nare positive integers, so $m-n=1$ and $m+n=1$. Subtracting gives\n$$\\begin{align*}2n&=1-1\\\\2n&=0\\\\n&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nThis contradicts $n>0$. Therefore no such positive integers exist.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      },
      {
        "id": "p12-q5-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "There exist real numbers $x$ and $y$ for which $2x^2+2y^2<3xy$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No such real numbers exist.\n\nCompleting the square gives $2x^2-3xy+2y^2=2\\left(x-\\frac34y\\right)^2+\\frac78y^2\\ge0$.",
          "worked": "For all real $x$ and $y$,\n$$\\begin{align*}2x^2-3xy+2y^2&=2\\left(x^2-\\frac32xy\\right)+2y^2\\\\2x^2-3xy+2y^2&=2\\left(x^2-\\frac32xy+\\frac9{16}y^2\\right)+\\frac78y^2\\\\2x^2-3xy+2y^2&=2\\left(x-\\frac34y\\right)^2+\\frac78y^2\\\\2x^2-3xy+2y^2&\\ge0.\\end{align*}$$\nBoth squared terms are nonnegative. The proposed inequality would require $2x^2-3xy+2y^2<0$, which is impossible. Therefore no such real numbers exist.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 62
      },
      {
        "id": "p12-q5-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "There exist integers $m$ and $n$ for which $m^2+n^2=105$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No such integers exist.\n\nChecking every possible square from $0$ to $100$ shows that none has a square complement to $105$.",
          "worked": "If $m^2+n^2=105$, each square is at most $105$. The possible values of $m^2$ are\n$$0,1,4,9,16,25,36,49,64,81,100.$$\nThe corresponding values of $105-m^2$ are\n$$105,104,101,96,89,80,69,56,41,24,5.$$\nNone is a perfect square, so none can equal $n^2$. Negative integers give the same squares as their positive counterparts. Therefore no such integers exist.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      },
      {
        "id": "p12-q5-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "There exist consecutive positive natural numbers whose product is a square number.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No such positive natural numbers exist.\n\nFor $n\\ge1$, $n^2<n(n+1)<(n+1)^2$, so the product lies strictly between consecutive squares.",
          "worked": "Write the consecutive positive natural numbers as $n$ and $n+1$, where $n\\ge1$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}n(n+1)-n^2&=n>0,\\\\(n+1)^2-n(n+1)&=n+1>0.\\end{align*}$$\nHence\n$$n^2<n(n+1)<(n+1)^2.$$\nThere is no square number strictly between these consecutive squares. Therefore no such positive natural numbers exist.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      }
    ]
  },
